lab/knowledge/official_README.md:88:- BUY\_SEED — Purchase N units of a single item from the market.  
lab/knowledge/official_README.md:95:- SELL — Sell N units of a single item to the market.  
lab/knowledge/official_README.md:175:Every product (and fertilizer) starts the game with a market inventory of `I0 = 10,000` units, far above any single game's realistic production volume so that inventory is essentially guaranteed to stay positive. The sell price for a product is `base` at `I0`, rises as inventory falls (players buying or town consumption draining supply), and falls as inventory grows (players selling).
lab/knowledge/official_README.md:203:`T` is the production capacity of a single 5×5 field over a 24-day game at optimal watering with no fertilizer (animal totals are pre-discounted by 30% to account for wheat-feed overhead). `target` says "moving `T` units past `I0` shifts the price by `target × base`." Picking different `f` and `target` on each side lets resources with similar production profiles play very differently strategically — wheat panics on scarcity but absorbs gluts, carrot is the opposite; melon barely reacts to scarcity but crashes hard on overproduction; wool mirrors melon at a smaller scale. Premium resources (base > $100: strawberry, melon, milk, wool) use `above_target > 1`, so even modest gluts drive them straight to the $1 floor — bundling and timing sales matters more for these than for staples.
